index d72c8c7..ce65953 100644
--- a/docs/business-rules.md
+++ b/docs/business-rules.md
@@ -96,9 +96,15 @@ ### Ticket
 
 **Issuance:** domain action `App\Actions\IssueTicket` rejects inactive queues (`is_active = false`), requires `priority >= 0`, allocates the next code, and creates a ticket with `status=waiting` and `issued_at` set.
 
+**Queue lifecycle (domain slice):** `App\Actions\CreateQueue` / `App\Actions\UpdateQueue` let the entity **owner/admin** create queues (name trimmed to 1–255 chars, slug defaults to `Str::slug(name)`), rename them and toggle `is_active`. Slugs are unique **within the entity** (duplicates — including race collisions on the DB index — surface as `ValidationException`); the same slug may exist in another entity. There is **no hard delete**: retiring a queue means deactivating it (`is_active = false`) — its tickets are preserved, and issuing or calling on it keeps failing with `InactiveQueueException`.
+
+**Per-queue access:** `queue_access` holds one row per (`entity_membership_id`, `queue_id`) with `permissions` as a JSON array of strings (today only `call`); both FKs cascade on delete. A manager (`App\Actions\SetQueueAccess`) upserts the row on a non-empty list and deletes it on an empty list (revoke); unknown or non-string entries are a `ValidationException` and store nothing. Every grant/revoke is audited on the `audit` log channel (`queue.access.granted` / `queue.access.revoked` with `actor_id`, `queue_id`, `entity_membership_id`); a revoke with no row logs nothing.
+
+**Authorization (single source of truth):** `App\Policies\QueuePolicy` — `create` (owner/admin of the entity), `update` / `manageAccess` (owner/admin of the queue's entity), `call` (owner/admin always; an employee only with a `queue_access` row for that queue containing `call`, a corrupted non-array value denies; everyone else denied). Both call paths (`App\Actions\CallNextTicket` after the active check, `Ticket::call()` after the inactive check and before the waiting check) enforce `call` and leave tickets untouched on `AuthorizationException`.
+
 **Immutability enforcement:** the write-once rule is enforced on the `Ticket` model (`updating` hook), so it applies to Eloquent saves only — mass updates through the query builder (`Ticket::query()->update(...)`) bypass it and must not be used on issued tickets.
 
-**Single-ticket call (scope note):** `Ticket::call()` marks one waiting ticket as `called` (entity-membership checked, exactly-once). This minimal domain primitive proves the mutable `status`/`called_*` fields. Deterministic call-next selection (priority + age + 3-ticket fairness) is implemented by `App\Actions\CallNextTicket` with the three call-panel modes below (`any` applies fairness; `priority_only`/`normal_only` ignore it); AuthZ stays out of scope per #20 and HTTP/Livewire panels remain future work.
+**Single-ticket call:** `Ticket::call()` marks one waiting ticket as `called` (exactly-once, queue locked and active-checked, caller authorized via `QueuePolicy@call`, then the waiting check). This minimal domain primitive proves the mutable `status`/`called_*` fields. Deterministic call-next selection (priority + age + 3-ticket fairness) is implemented by `App\Actions\CallNextTicket` with the three call-panel modes below (`any` applies fairness; `priority_only`/`normal_only` ignore it); HTTP/Livewire panels remain future work.
 
 ### Priority
 
